const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why does MC1R cause red hair?',
    a: 'MC1R on melanocytes chooses the pigment. Strong signalling makes brown-black eumelanin. Loss-of-function variants weaken that signal, so the cell makes more reddish pheomelanin instead. The common strong variants are R151C, R160W and D294H.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is an R allele?',
    a: '“R” is the shorthand for a strong loss-of-function MC1R allele — enough, especially as two copies, to produce red hair, fair skin that burns, and freckles. “r” means a weak allele, such as V60L, V92M or R163Q, with a smaller, dose-dependent effect. One strong allele often shows up as freckling and sun sensitivity without red hair.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do red-hair variants raise melanoma risk beyond fair skin?',
    a: 'Yes. Carrying these variants raises melanoma risk even after accounting for hair colour and complexion. Pheomelanin is a poor sunscreen and can itself feed oxidative damage, and a working MC1R also supports DNA repair in the melanocyte. The risk is real; it is not a diagnosis.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is red hair in POMC deficiency the same thing?',
    a: 'Same pigment, different break. In red-hair MC1R variants the receptor is faulty and the ligand is present. In POMC deficiency the receptor is intact and the ligand was never made, so the person has red hair together with obesity and adrenal failure. Setmelanotide replaces the appetite signal; it does not repair MC1R.',
  },
];

