<PageLayout
  title="Red hair and MC1R: the R alleles, freckles, and a pigment that burns"
  seoTitle="Red hair, MC1R and the R alleles"
  kicker="The pigment switch"
  description="Red hair, fair skin that burns, and freckles are the visible result of loss-of-function MC1R variants — the strong “R” alleles, classically R151C, R160W and D294H. Weakened signalling shifts melanocytes from protective eumelanin to pheomelanin. The same alleles raise melanoma risk beyond complexion alone, and they are a different break from the red hair of POMC deficiency."
  seoDescription="Red hair and freckles come from loss-of-function MC1R alleles, especially R151C, R160W and D294H. They shift pigment to pheomelanin and raise melanoma risk."
  thread="One receptor on the melanocyte decides whether the pigment is a sunscreen or a liability. The common variants do not break the cell. They turn the switch down."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Red hair', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_hair'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'MC1R is the receptor on melanocytes that chooses the pigment: eumelanin when the signal is strong, pheomelanin when it is weak.',
      'The strong loss-of-function alleles — the “R” series, classically R151C, R160W and D294H — are the genetic core of red hair, sun-sensitive fair skin and freckling.',
      'Weaker “r” alleles (V60L, V92M, R163Q) shift the same traits modestly. One strong allele often freckles without reddening the hair; two strong alleles are the usual red-hair genotype.',
      'Several R alleles fail by never reaching the cell surface in normal amounts, so α-MSH is present and still cannot signal.',
      'The same variants raise melanoma risk beyond what fair skin explains. A separate page covers the pain and anaesthesia findings. POMC deficiency causes red hair by a different break: no ligand, not a broken receptor.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> is the pigment receptor. <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>,
    cut from <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>, lands on it and the melanocyte makes brown-black
    eumelanin, the pigment that absorbs ultraviolet light. Turn that signal down and the
    cell makes reddish-yellow <strong>pheomelanin</strong> instead. Red hair, skin that
    burns rather than tans, and freckles are what that choice looks like from the
    outside.<Cite n={4} />
  </p>

  <h2>R alleles and r alleles</h2>
  <p>
    The association is thirty years old. In 1995, variants of the melanocyte-stimulating
    hormone receptor were tied to red hair and fair skin in people.<Cite n={1} /> MC1R has
    turned out to be one of the most polymorphic genes in the genome, so the field sorts
    the common coding changes into two grades rather than listing every one as equal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>R alleles</strong> are strong loss-of-function. The classic three are Arg151Cys
    (R151C), Arg160Trp (R160W) and Asp294His (D294H). Asp84Glu, Arg142His and Ile155Thr sit
    with them: uncommon, and high-penetrance when present.<Cite n={2} /> Two R alleles is
    the usual genotype behind unmistakably red hair. One R allele is commoner than red hair
    itself and more often shows up as freckling, a lighter complexion and a tendency to
    burn, with hair that may be auburn, blond or brown.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>r alleles</strong> are partial. Val60Leu (V60L), Val92Met (V92M) and Arg163Gln
    (R163Q) reduce signalling modestly, and the phenotype is dose-dependent and easy to
    miss.<Cite n={2} /> R163Q is the common allele in many East Asian populations, and its
    link to pigmentation and skin cancer has been reported both ways — a reminder that
    “MC1R variant” is not one thing. The graded list, with frequencies and mechanisms, is
    on the <a href="/genetics">genetics</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>How a variant actually fails</h2>
  <p>
    Several of the strong alleles do not simply bind α-MSH and then refuse to signal. They
    are mis-trafficked: less receptor reaches the cell surface, so the ligand is there and
    the lock is not.<Cite n={3} /> That is why the phenotype can be close to a loss of the
    gene even though a protein is made. The natural brake on the same receptor,{' '}
    <a href="/asip">agouti signalling protein</a>, produces a related pigment shift in other
    mammals by blocking a receptor that is otherwise intact. Human red hair is mostly the
    receptor’s own variants, not an excess of that brake.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pheomelanin is a poor sunscreen, and it can generate reactive oxygen species rather than
    only failing to block them. A working MC1R does more than pick a pigment: the cAMP
    signal it raises also supports DNA repair and antioxidant defences inside the
    melanocyte. Take both away and the melanoma risk of R alleles is larger than fair skin
    and red hair alone would predict.<Cite n={4} /> Carrying them is a risk factor. It is
    not a diagnosis, and it is not destiny — ultraviolet dose still dominates what happens
    to any given person.
  </p>

  <h2>The same alleles, outside the skin</h2>
  <p>
    MC1R is not only a pigment gene. The same loss-of-function variants track with a
    measurable difference in pain and anaesthesia: more inhaled anaesthetic to prevent
    movement, less effect from injected lidocaine, and, in the other direction, more relief
    from certain opioids. That evidence has its own page,{' '}
    <a href="/mc1r-pain">red hair, MC1R and pain</a>, because it is a nervous-system finding
    and not a pigment one.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second way to get red hair from this pathway, and it is worth not confusing
    with the first. In <a href="/pomc-deficiency">POMC deficiency</a> the receptor is
    normal and the ligand was never cut from the precursor. The person has red hair because
    no α-MSH arrives, together with early obesity and adrenal failure, because the same
    missing precursor was supposed to supply the appetite signal and ACTH. The R alleles
    leave appetite and the adrenal alone. They only turn down the pigment receptor.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Red hair is the human phenotype that made MC1R famous, and it is still the cleanest
    illustration of what the receptor is for: a switch between two pigments, set by how
    much signal gets through. The R alleles are common, graded, and consequential for sun
    and for melanoma risk. They are also the wrong explanation for every redhead in a
    textbook. For the receptor itself, see <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>; for the
    peptide that normally flips it, <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>; for the drug that
    activates the same receptor to build a protective tan in porphyria,{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--accent-soft)] p-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page explains MC1R variant biology. It is not medical advice, a genetic-test
      interpretation, or a sun-protection plan. Skin-cancer screening belongs with a
      clinician. See the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

